re-wysiwyg-editor/commit/5880d564304088b033914f0eb364634bf768c4f9","sha":"5880d564304088b033914f0eb364634bf768c4f9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/5880d564304088b033914f0eb364634bf768c4f9"}],"sha":"34e750fdd64227ecbe68b7ee3cca16bc5c0419f2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/34e750fdd64227ecbe68b7ee3cca16bc5c0419f2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/104770750?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/robertSt7","id":104770750,"login":"robertSt7","node_id":"U_kgDOBj6svg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/5880d564304088b033914f0eb364634bf768c4f9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-28T13:33:48Z","email":"104770750+robertSt7@users.noreply.github.com","name":"robertSt7"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-28T13:33:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Remove enterprise subscription tools and relicense under POCL (#31)\n\n* Remove enterprise subscription tools, make the bundle community edition\n\nThe bundle is licensed under GPLv3 (LICENSE.md) and must not depend on the\nenterprise stack:\n\n- drop pimcore/enterprise-subscription-tools and the enterprise composer\n  repository from composer.json\n- stop implementing EnterpriseBundleInterface, registering\n  PimcoreEnterpriseSubscriptionToolsBundle and returning the placeholder\n  license id in PimcoreWysiwygEditor\n- set the composer and npm license to GPL-3.0-or-later (was PEL/proprietary)\n- drop the PEL mention from the headers of the CI scaffolding files\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Relicense the bundle under the Pimcore Open Core License (POCL)\n\n- replace the GPLv3 LICENSE.md with the POCL text used by the other\n  Pimcore bundles\n- switch all PHP and TypeScript file headers, and the php-cs-fixer\n  header_comment rule, to the POCL header\n- set the composer and npm license field to \"proprietary\", as in the\n  other POCL bundles\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Add the POCL header to the remaining PHP files\n\nbundles.php (CI scaffolding) and phpstan-bootstrap.php had no license\nheader at all, so the header switch missed them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"6ac99c05bdfeb5e3027bfe89d87930fd39650ca0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/git/trees/6ac99c05bdfeb5e3027bfe89d87930fd39650ca0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/git/commits/5880d564304088b033914f0eb364634bf768c4f9","verification":{"payload":"tree 6ac99c05bdfeb5e3027bfe89d87930fd39650ca0\nparent 612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4\nauthor robertSt7 <104770750+robertSt7@users.noreply.github.com> 1790602428 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790602428 +0200\n\nRemove enterprise subscription tools and relicense under POCL (#31)\n\n* Remove enterprise subscription tools, make the bundle community edition\n\nThe bundle is licensed under GPLv3 (LICENSE.md) and must not depend on the\nenterprise stack:\n\n- drop pimcore/enterprise-subscription-tools and the enterprise composer\n  repository from composer.json\n- stop implementing EnterpriseBundleInterface, registering\n  PimcoreEnterpriseSubscriptionToolsBundle and returning the placeholder\n  license id in PimcoreWysiwygEditor\n- set the composer and npm license to GPL-3.0-or-later (was PEL/proprietary)\n- drop the PEL mention from the headers of the CI scaffolding files\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Relicense the bundle under the Pimcore Open Core License (POCL)\n\n- replace the GPLv3 LICENSE.md with the POCL text used by the other\n  Pimcore bundles\n- switch all PHP and TypeScript file headers, and the php-cs-fixer\n  header_comment rule, to the POCL header\n- set the composer and npm license field to \"proprietary\", as in the\n  other POCL bundles\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Add the POCL header to the remaining PHP files\n\nbundles.php (CI scaffolding) and phpstan-bootstrap.php had no license\nheader at all, so the header switch missed them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqumy8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAy/IQAJwYoBQg+m0F+i3K0wPE/mY7\nktfz1SFGSNbRscjJxVEh+UKJxkq1nBG+QhRI4hG8uzfGTw/x4PtiU4BDQFVVBAZ8\n4nGRijQy8Kxpa85EpmFmgZru+5fF6nb/JR9TIGWMhjWQKSMghV4fEVGhdzyEitaY\nx1pMzmUqCQzEGadvabvI2JsDXHU4uOKpzxW/obeCNRhQYD49XF2kP8EQms43Zl2Q\n/HdHt4o3KO+qtXpO5UpNuLvMS2XWsTx8cXXG35b3WaPYpQHNqzQzKS3KHFBaoRXl\nrN1CzUOHZyGAOUTLXP/waRjevdXfF+bU9gaz+eVhLQygYnsVxHqZeZhf0j0YBdMs\n2XEU1eXVOACKUtQyeF1LdS3VeUdhpjvST75KXGlVrrsJpnhkiPS7Y23slBe9u0/j\nffvDU2+fHJ90gcJRde3daQpUvGCC0YwSRw2NiMChogEdei741QpKKr5vsaq+1Tb7\nu9Rln3RuU8tXTkmC0HfGcHbHBfBea7a0Lu1GsAKPCUbgwwKKy8ltG5a91fLjQKbH\nRAaz+7quWeP0nzfC1tSBATU4E3gM7MAH4/wuZr08CWPnnfO0CcXo9w2zJu6SeZ6v\ntXGDqY44Cwz1D3XsAya41TmxZjaVoVRrIKAZ+TVV42AX/XHwkeC6yfvLbHk6lEHo\nTEKS+5ybE5WGOHvdiyNP\n=HS2M\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-28T13:33:49Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commit/5880d564304088b033914f0eb364634bf768c4f9","node_id":"C_kwDOT_fY69oAKDU4ODBkNTY0MzA0MDg4YjAzMzkxNGYwZWIzNjQ2MzRiZjc2OGM0Zjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commit/612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4","sha":"612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4"}],"sha":"5880d564304088b033914f0eb364634bf768c4f9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/5880d564304088b033914f0eb364634bf768c4f9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/104770750?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/robertSt7","id":104770750,"login":"robertSt7","node_id":"U_kgDOBj6svg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/robertSt7","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T16:52:48Z","email":"104770750+robertSt7@users.noreply.github.com","name":"robertSt7"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T16:52:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Stop bold/italic promoting an adjacent space to nbsp (#29)\n\n* Stop bold/italic promoting an adjacent space to nbsp\n\nVerified directly against real Chrome: wrapping a selection turns\nwhatever sits immediately beside the result into &nbsp; — a plain space\ntouching either boundary, wherever the selection sits within a longer\nrun of text, or an empty text node the browser leaves behind when there\nwas nothing on that side at all (the whole field''s content selected).\nMarking only part of a word never touches a space boundary, which is\nwhy that case was never reported as broken.\n\nThe wrap now records what sat on each side of the selection before\ntouching the DOM, and afterwards puts back exactly that — a plain space\nback to a plain space, nothing back to nothing. An nbsp the field\nalready held, or an ordinary character, was never at risk and is left\nexactly as it is.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Handle an element-boundary selection, and a marker collision\n\nSelecting a whole element rather than a run of text gives a range\nboundary that indexes into an element''s children, not a character\noffset into a text node. The boundary check read the wrong node for\nthat shape, walking the element''s own siblings instead of the specific\nchild living at that index — which meant an nbsp already sitting next\nto the selection could be read as nothing being there at all, and then\nremoved as if the wrap had invented it.\n\nSeparately, the temporary attribute marking the freshly wrapped element\nwas a fixed name only, so field content already carrying that exact\nattribute — persisted through the code view, say — could be picked up\nby the lookup instead of what this operation just inserted. Each call\nnow carries a unique value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Build the wrap on a detached clone, commit it in one step\n\nThe previous fix corrected a browser''s own insertHTML behaviour after\nthe fact by inspecting and patching the DOM it produced. Two more real\ngaps followed from working that way: `execCommand` boundaries index an\nelement''s children when the selection is a whole element rather than a\nrun of text, which the correction read past entirely and could strip a\ngenuine nbsp sitting there; and locating what execCommand had just\ninserted depended on a marker attribute a project could already be\npersisting elsewhere, in which case the wrong element got corrected.\nWorse, the correction ran as a plain DOM mutation after execCommand had\nalready returned, so it sat outside the browser''s own undo entry —\nredoing the edit could replay the browser''s raw, uncorrected result.\n\nWrapping the selection through the plain Range API on a clone, detached\nfrom the document, sidesteps the browser behaviour being corrected for\nentirely — confirmed directly: replacing a range this way never pads a\nboundary the way `execCommand(''insertHTML'')` does. Committing the whole,\nalready-correct clone through the existing `mutateWithHistory` helper —\nalready used for a link''s address and a list item''s block — makes it\none undo step to begin with, so a following redo replays that same\ncorrect result. There is no longer a DOM state to read back and\ncorrect, so the whole boundary-character machinery goes with it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the caret in place, and split a wrap across blocks\n\nTwo gaps followed from replacing the whole field''s content in one step\nto keep the wrap and its correction as a single undo entry: execCommand\nleaves the selection at the end of whatever it just inserted, which for\na whole-field replacement is the end of the field, so the caret jumped\naway from the word that was just bolded rather than landing right after\nit; and wrapping an extracted range in one inline element assumed the\nselection stayed within a single block, which a selection spanning two\nparagraphs does not — the extracted fragment carries partial copies of\neach paragraph with it, and wrapping that in a `<b>` nests block content\ninside an inline element no parser can make sense of.\n\n`mutateWithHistory` now accepts a path back from its caller to the node\nthe caret belongs after, resolved against the live document once the\ncommit lands, since a path computed for the clone still applies to the\nsame target once its content is replaced with that clone''s. The wrap\nitself now splits across a text node''s nearest block-level ancestor\nwhen the selection''s start and end fall in different ones, one wrapper\nper block, so bolding a run spanning two paragraphs bolds each\nparagraph''s part of it independently, exactly as a native editor would.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Split a wrap correctly for every shape a selection can take\n\nThree gaps in how the wrap decided where block boundaries fall:\n\nSelecting a whole field gives a range whose boundaries index the field''s\nown children rather than sitting inside any one block, so nothing was\nrecognised as crossed and every paragraph went into a single inline\nelement. The boundaries are now resolved to the leaf positions they\nactually reach before any block is tested against them, and a single\nblock both ends share has its own edges used in place of a range that\nwould otherwise wrap that block''s element rather than the text in it.\n\nDIV, UL and OL were missing from the block list. DIV is what the\nbrowser''s own commands fall back to and what code view content can use;\nUL and OL matter because without them a range crossing a nested list\nresolved to the LI inside it, whose parent is that list rather than the\nelement holding the runs either side.\n\nA selection whose ends share a block but which spans a nested one — a\nlist inside a list item — wrapped that list inside the inline element.\nEach crossed block is now wrapped separately, recursing into one the\nrange covers entirely, and a block nested inside one already collected\nis left to that recursion rather than being wrapped twice.\n\nTwo lower-level faults surfaced while fixing those: a live Range\nre-anchors its boundaries as soon as a node it points into is removed,\nso the boundaries are read once up front; and extractContents leaves an\nempty text node behind, shifting sibling indices computed afterwards, so\neach parent is normalised before its indices are read again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Treat anything not known to be inline as a block, and clamp segments\n\nListing which tags are blocks left out everything the code view can\nbring in that the toolbar never builds — a table, a pre, a section — so\nformatting across one could put a cell or a row inside a b/i. The list\nis inverted: the inline tags a b/i may legally wrap are named, and\nanything else counts as a block the wrap has to stay inside.\n\nA whole-field selection also starts and ends at the field itself, one\nlevel out from the container the first and last crossed blocks sit in,\nso the leading and trailing segments spanned that gap and extracted a\npartial copy of the container between them — a stray empty b/i either\nside of a field that is just a list. Both segments are now clamped to\nthe container the block they border actually lives in, which collapses\nthem away when there was nothing in between to format.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Skip a wrap with nothing in it, and repair the draft before committing\n\nA range holding no text still reached the wrap — an empty block, or a\nvoid element a b/i may not enclose. The empty wrapper that produced is\ndropped again by the reparse on the way in, leaving the caret position\npointing at a node no longer there. Such a range is now left alone, and\nthe caret is only moved when a wrapper was actually created.\n\nCommitting the clone reinserts the whole field, and a list the browser\nleaves inside a paragraph does not survive that reparse: a list item\nends up orphaned beside the list. That shape is exactly what the stored\nvalue is already repaired for, so the same repair now runs on the clone\nbefore it is committed, inside the one undoable step. The wrap returns\nits wrapper rather than a path so the caret still lands correctly after\nthat repair moves nodes around.\n\nThe jsdom stand-in for execCommand now reproduces what Chrome was\nmeasured doing — promoting a plain space beside a replacement to\n&nbsp; — so taking the fix back out fails the tests rather than passing\nthem unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the caret where it is when a committed path leads nowhere\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep text typed after a freshly marked word plain\n\nRestoring the caret right after the new b/i element leaves it, to the\nbrowser''s typing style, still inside that element: everything typed\nnext came out bold or italic as well. Switch the typing style off again\nonce the caret is in place, using the same command the collapsed branch\nuses to switch it on — except where the surroundings already render the\nmark by themselves, a heading''s own weight say, since there \"off\" makes\nthe browser wrap the next characters in a span{font-weight:normal}\ninstead of leaving them plain.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Write back only the blocks a mark touches, never the whole field\n\nCommitting the finished wrap by replacing the whole field''s contents\nleft it to the browser''s insertHTML, which treats whatever the replaced\nrange begins with as the block to reinsert into: a field opening with a\nheading or a list kept that block as a shell and pulled everything after\nit inside — a paragraph rendered as a heading, a paragraph swallowed by\na list. Measured against Chromium directly, including the shapes that\nstay clean: a range starting inside a block, or with inline nodes, is\nwritten back as given.\n\nCommit the contents of the one block both ends of the selection sit in;\nwhen there is none, commit each top-level block or inline run the\nselection reaches on its own, last to first so indices stay valid. A\nfield beginning with inline nodes is still written back whole — it has\nno block to become a shell, and its inline run written back alone picks\nup a stray line break before the next block. Cross-block selections\ntake one undo step per block in exchange for never corrupting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Commit units inside the shared block, and unwrap the same way\n\nThe block both ends of a selection share was written back whole, so a\nblock whose own first child is a block — a div holding a heading and\ntwo paragraphs, a paragraph the browser left a list in — began the\nreplaced range with that block and hit the same shell behaviour the\nfield-level commit had. The children of the shared block are now split\ninto units the same way the field''s are, so nothing written back ever\nstarts with a block it does not belong to.\n\nToggling a mark off still replaced the live element itself through\ninsertHTML, which promoted the plain spaces on either side of it to\n&nbsp; — measured directly, and at a paragraph end it even wrapped the\ntext in a font-size span. Unwrapping goes through the same scoped commit\nas wrapping now.\n\nWhen the last selected unit yields nothing to place the caret after, the\ncaret ends at the end of that unit rather than after a wrapper in an\nearlier one. The test stub promotes the boundary spaces before the range\nis mutated, so a selection within one text node exercises both sides.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Clone only the span being written back, and split blocks all the way down\n\nThe clone a change was made on held the whole container, so the list\nrepair could move nodes in front of the span and shift its indices —\nthe written-back run would then have copied unrelated siblings. The\nclone now holds the span alone: nothing outside it exists to be moved,\nand the caret anchor is found again by offsetting the clone''s path by\nthe span''s start.\n\nA block the selection reached was written back by its own contents even\nwhen it began with a block of its own, which hands the browser a range\nstarting with that block — the shell case, one level down. Blocks are\nsplit into spans recursively now, so no span written back ever starts\nwith a block. The typing-style query goes through the guarded helper,\nso a document that cannot answer it still gets the change emitted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Skip spans a mark left unchanged, and keep the unwrap caret in place\n\nA span the change did not touch — a void element, an empty block at\nthe end of a selection — was still written back, adding an undo step\nwith nothing in it that had to be undone before the formatting itself.\nIt is left alone now; the caret still ends at that span.\n\nUnwrapping a mark set its text free between two other text nodes, and\nthe three come back from the commit as one — so the node the caret was\nto follow no longer existed and the caret fell to the end of the block.\nThe position is kept as an offset into that run instead. A paragraph\nthe browser left a list in after its text is written back whole on\npurpose: measured against Chromium, it lifts the list out itself, while\nthe text run written back alone picks up a stray line break.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Settle a block''s children without merging, and treat block styling like a heading''s\n\nSettling the empty text node a leading wrap leaves behind used\nnormalize(), which recurses and merges every adjacent pair of text\nnodes below the block — including one the range''s own end still pointed\ninto, after which the end read as outside the block and the whole rest\nof it was formatted. Only the empty direct children are dropped now.\n\nAn element carrying the mark as an inline style counted as the existing\nmark whatever it was, so a paragraph styled bold was found as the mark\nto take off — a block that can neither be located inside the span being\nwritten back nor sensibly unwrapped. Block-level styling is treated the\nway a heading''s own weight already is: the text sits in a bold block,\nthe button adds the inline mark inside it, and only inline elements are\nunwrapped. Oblique counts as inherited italic styling as well.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Cover a nested list in a paragraph the browser left it in after the text\n\nMeasured against Chromium end to end: written back whole, the list is\nlifted out of the paragraph with its nesting intact, for a properly\nnested list as much as for the list-beside-item shape indent produces.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Keep the field itself out of a list repair, and settle the last review items\n\nA list item sitting directly in the field — source-view HTML can put\none there — makes the field the \"list\" the Backspace repair writes\nback, and selecting the field element itself replaced it. The field\nonly ever has its contents replaced again.\n\nA boundary sitting in an empty text node about to be dropped is moved\nto the same position in the parent first, counted in the children that\nremain. Styled phrasing elements the inline list lacked (del, ins,\nlabel) are inline; an oblique inline style is an italic mark as much as\na computed one is; collecting crossed blocks checks only the block\ncollected last, the only one that can still contain the current one.\nThe test stand-in promotes the space beside an element replaced whole —\nthe trailing one, as measured — so the unwrap test tells the routes\napart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c565d86bc6135f34d988ad1db87c46ab726698b7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/git/trees/c565d86bc6135f34d988ad1db87c46ab726698b7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/git/commits/612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4","verification":{"payload":"tree c565d86bc6135f34d988ad1db87c46ab726698b7\nparent f56c7dfe7e3a31551393e64e99138f2929eb0727\nauthor robertSt7 <104770750+robertSt7@users.noreply.github.com> 1790009568 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790009568 +0200\n\nStop bold/italic promoting an adjacent space to nbsp (#29)\n\n* Stop bold/italic promoting an adjacent space to nbsp\n\nVerified directly against real Chrome: wrapping a selection turns\nwhatever sits immediately beside the result into &nbsp; — a plain space\ntouching either boundary, wherever the selection sits within a longer\nrun of text, or an empty text node the browser leaves behind when there\nwas nothing on that side at all (the whole field''s content selected).\nMarking only part of a word never touches a space boundary, which is\nwhy that case was never reported as broken.\n\nThe wrap now records what sat on each side of the selection before\ntouching the DOM, and afterwards puts back exactly that — a plain space\nback to a plain space, nothing back to nothing. An nbsp the field\nalready held, or an ordinary character, was never at risk and is left\nexactly as it is.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Handle an element-boundary selection, and a marker collision\n\nSelecting a whole element rather than a run of text gives a range\nboundary that indexes into an element''s children, not a character\noffset into a text node. The boundary check read the wrong node for\nthat shape, walking the element''s own siblings instead of the specific\nchild living at that index — which meant an nbsp already sitting next\nto the selection could be read as nothing being there at all, and then\nremoved as if the wrap had invented it.\n\nSeparately, the temporary attribute marking the freshly wrapped element\nwas a fixed name only, so field content already carrying that exact\nattribute — persisted through the code view, say — could be picked up\nby the lookup instead of what this operation just inserted. Each call\nnow carries a unique value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Build the wrap on a detached clone, commit it in one step\n\nThe previous fix corrected a browser''s own insertHTML behaviour after\nthe fact by inspecting and patching the DOM it produced. Two more real\ngaps followed from working that way: `execCommand` boundaries index an\nelement''s children when the selection is a whole element rather than a\nrun of text, which the correction read past entirely and could strip a\ngenuine nbsp sitting there; and locating what execCommand had just\ninserted depended on a marker attribute a project could already be\npersisting elsewhere, in which case the wrong element got corrected.\nWorse, the correction ran as a plain DOM mutation after execCommand had\nalready returned, so it sat outside the browser''s own undo entry —\nredoing the edit could replay the browser''s raw, uncorrected result.\n\nWrapping the selection through the plain Range API on a clone, detached\nfrom the document, sidesteps the browser behaviour being corrected for\nentirely — confirmed directly: replacing a range this way never pads a\nboundary the way `execCommand(''insertHTML'')` does. Committing the whole,\nalready-correct clone through the existing `mutateWithHistory` helper —\nalready used for a link''s address and a list item''s block — makes it\none undo step to begin with, so a following redo replays that same\ncorrect result. There is no longer a DOM state to read back and\ncorrect, so the whole boundary-character machinery goes with it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the caret in place, and split a wrap across blocks\n\nTwo gaps followed from replacing the whole field''s content in one step\nto keep the wrap and its correction as a single undo entry: execCommand\nleaves the selection at the end of whatever it just inserted, which for\na whole-field replacement is the end of the field, so the caret jumped\naway from the word that was just bolded rather than landing right after\nit; and wrapping an extracted range in one inline element assumed the\nselection stayed within a single block, which a selection spanning two\nparagraphs does not — the extracted fragment carries partial copies of\neach paragraph with it, and wrapping that in a `<b>` nests block content\ninside an inline element no parser can make sense of.\n\n`mutateWithHistory` now accepts a path back from its caller to the node\nthe caret belongs after, resolved against the live document once the\ncommit lands, since a path computed for the clone still applies to the\nsame target once its content is replaced with that clone''s. The wrap\nitself now splits across a text node''s nearest block-level ancestor\nwhen the selection''s start and end fall in different ones, one wrapper\nper block, so bolding a run spanning two paragraphs bolds each\nparagraph''s part of it independently, exactly as a native editor would.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Split a wrap correctly for every shape a selection can take\n\nThree gaps in how the wrap decided where block boundaries fall:\n\nSelecting a whole field gives a range whose boundaries index the field''s\nown children rather than sitting inside any one block, so nothing was\nrecognised as crossed and every paragraph went into a single inline\nelement. The boundaries are now resolved to the leaf positions they\nactually reach before any block is tested against them, and a single\nblock both ends share has its own edges used in place of a range that\nwould otherwise wrap that block''s element rather than the text in it.\n\nDIV, UL and OL were missing from the block list. DIV is what the\nbrowser''s own commands fall back to and what code view content can use;\nUL and OL matter because without them a range crossing a nested list\nresolved to the LI inside it, whose parent is that list rather than the\nelement holding the runs either side.\n\nA selection whose ends share a block but which spans a nested one — a\nlist inside a list item — wrapped that list inside the inline element.\nEach crossed block is now wrapped separately, recursing into one the\nrange covers entirely, and a block nested inside one already collected\nis left to that recursion rather than being wrapped twice.\n\nTwo lower-level faults surfaced while fixing those: a live Range\nre-anchors its boundaries as soon as a node it points into is removed,\nso the boundaries are read once up front; and extractContents leaves an\nempty text node behind, shifting sibling indices computed afterwards, so\neach parent is normalised before its indices are read again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Treat anything not known to be inline as a block, and clamp segments\n\nListing which tags are blocks left out everything the code view can\nbring in that the toolbar never builds — a table, a pre, a section — so\nformatting across one could put a cell or a row inside a b/i. The list\nis inverted: the inline tags a b/i may legally wrap are named, and\nanything else counts as a block the wrap has to stay inside.\n\nA whole-field selection also starts and ends at the field itself, one\nlevel out from the container the first and last crossed blocks sit in,\nso the leading and trailing segments spanned that gap and extracted a\npartial copy of the container between them — a stray empty b/i either\nside of a field that is just a list. Both segments are now clamped to\nthe container the block they border actually lives in, which collapses\nthem away when there was nothing in between to format.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Skip a wrap with nothing in it, and repair the draft before committing\n\nA range holding no text still reached the wrap — an empty block, or a\nvoid element a b/i may not enclose. The empty wrapper that produced is\ndropped again by the reparse on the way in, leaving the caret position\npointing at a node no longer there. Such a range is now left alone, and\nthe caret is only moved when a wrapper was actually created.\n\nCommitting the clone reinserts the whole field, and a list the browser\nleaves inside a paragraph does not survive that reparse: a list item\nends up orphaned beside the list. That shape is exactly what the stored\nvalue is already repaired for, so the same repair now runs on the clone\nbefore it is committed, inside the one undoable step. The wrap returns\nits wrapper rather than a path so the caret still lands correctly after\nthat repair moves nodes around.\n\nThe jsdom stand-in for execCommand now reproduces what Chrome was\nmeasured doing — promoting a plain space beside a replacement to\n&nbsp; — so taking the fix back out fails the tests rather than passing\nthem unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the caret where it is when a committed path leads nowhere\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep text typed after a freshly marked word plain\n\nRestoring the caret right after the new b/i element leaves it, to the\nbrowser''s typing style, still inside that element: everything typed\nnext came out bold or italic as well. Switch the typing style off again\nonce the caret is in place, using the same command the collapsed branch\nuses to switch it on — except where the surroundings already render the\nmark by themselves, a heading''s own weight say, since there \"off\" makes\nthe browser wrap the next characters in a span{font-weight:normal}\ninstead of leaving them plain.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Write back only the blocks a mark touches, never the whole field\n\nCommitting the finished wrap by replacing the whole field''s contents\nleft it to the browser''s insertHTML, which treats whatever the replaced\nrange begins with as the block to reinsert into: a field opening with a\nheading or a list kept that block as a shell and pulled everything after\nit inside — a paragraph rendered as a heading, a paragraph swallowed by\na list. Measured against Chromium directly, including the shapes that\nstay clean: a range starting inside a block, or with inline nodes, is\nwritten back as given.\n\nCommit the contents of the one block both ends of the selection sit in;\nwhen there is none, commit each top-level block or inline run the\nselection reaches on its own, last to first so indices stay valid. A\nfield beginning with inline nodes is still written back whole — it has\nno block to become a shell, and its inline run written back alone picks\nup a stray line break before the next block. Cross-block selections\ntake one undo step per block in exchange for never corrupting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Commit units inside the shared block, and unwrap the same way\n\nThe block both ends of a selection share was written back whole, so a\nblock whose own first child is a block — a div holding a heading and\ntwo paragraphs, a paragraph the browser left a list in — began the\nreplaced range with that block and hit the same shell behaviour the\nfield-level commit had. The children of the shared block are now split\ninto units the same way the field''s are, so nothing written back ever\nstarts with a block it does not belong to.\n\nToggling a mark off still replaced the live element itself through\ninsertHTML, which promoted the plain spaces on either side of it to\n&nbsp; — measured directly, and at a paragraph end it even wrapped the\ntext in a font-size span. Unwrapping goes through the same scoped commit\nas wrapping now.\n\nWhen the last selected unit yields nothing to place the caret after, the\ncaret ends at the end of that unit rather than after a wrapper in an\nearlier one. The test stub promotes the boundary spaces before the range\nis mutated, so a selection within one text node exercises both sides.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Clone only the span being written back, and split blocks all the way down\n\nThe clone a change was made on held the whole container, so the list\nrepair could move nodes in front of the span and shift its indices —\nthe written-back run would then have copied unrelated siblings. The\nclone now holds the span alone: nothing outside it exists to be moved,\nand the caret anchor is found again by offsetting the clone''s path by\nthe span''s start.\n\nA block the selection reached was written back by its own contents even\nwhen it began with a block of its own, which hands the browser a range\nstarting with that block — the shell case, one level down. Blocks are\nsplit into spans recursively now, so no span written back ever starts\nwith a block. The typing-style query goes through the guarded helper,\nso a document that cannot answer it still gets the change emitted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Skip spans a mark left unchanged, and keep the unwrap caret in place\n\nA span the change did not touch — a void element, an empty block at\nthe end of a selection — was still written back, adding an undo step\nwith nothing in it that had to be undone before the formatting itself.\nIt is left alone now; the caret still ends at that span.\n\nUnwrapping a mark set its text free between two other text nodes, and\nthe three come back from the commit as one — so the node the caret was\nto follow no longer existed and the caret fell to the end of the block.\nThe position is kept as an offset into that run instead. A paragraph\nthe browser left a list in after its text is written back whole on\npurpose: measured against Chromium, it lifts the list out itself, while\nthe text run written back alone picks up a stray line break.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Settle a block''s children without merging, and treat block styling like a heading''s\n\nSettling the empty text node a leading wrap leaves behind used\nnormalize(), which recurses and merges every adjacent pair of text\nnodes below the block — including one the range''s own end still pointed\ninto, after which the end read as outside the block and the whole rest\nof it was formatted. Only the empty direct children are dropped now.\n\nAn element carrying the mark as an inline style counted as the existing\nmark whatever it was, so a paragraph styled bold was found as the mark\nto take off — a block that can neither be located inside the span being\nwritten back nor sensibly unwrapped. Block-level styling is treated the\nway a heading''s own weight already is: the text sits in a bold block,\nthe button adds the inline mark inside it, and only inline elements are\nunwrapped. Oblique counts as inherited italic styling as well.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Cover a nested list in a paragraph the browser left it in after the text\n\nMeasured against Chromium end to end: written back whole, the list is\nlifted out of the paragraph with its nesting intact, for a properly\nnested list as much as for the list-beside-item shape indent produces.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n* Keep the field itself out of a list repair, and settle the last review items\n\nA list item sitting directly in the field — source-view HTML can put\none there — makes the field the \"list\" the Backspace repair writes\nback, and selecting the field element itself replaced it. The field\nonly ever has its contents replaced again.\n\nA boundary sitting in an empty text node about to be dropped is moved\nto the same position in the parent first, counted in the children that\nremain. Styled phrasing elements the inline list lacked (del, ins,\nlabel) are inline; an oblique inline style is an italic mark as much as\na computed one is; collecting crossed blocks checks only the block\ncollected last, the only one that can still contain the current one.\nThe test stand-in promotes the space beside an element replaced whole —\nthe trailing one, as measured — so the unwrap test tells the routes\napart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Automatic frontend build\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsWDgCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAuJoQAB7qRT1RdI8PSDjfEra0LRQL\nG2Ef/TRTOVoBYyDqRrXdd7Lfvxi6JilKnfsN+vPycMpLRByxTCEeBGF0C72uZJP2\nAe73u6HdLNCl+ibQbQ3hS5vcU/HcfSi54w1I2SFZOt1P2Gq65hSNd72ND0C4T+np\n1hqB389KHBxb27aJqzKt/rU2Vl7tRgLIuzm2Rc/Q3NyvSlXBWBiF0UtvFuPfGTsj\n22Z06yaTR/ouQxTxUeUOp1Uifwl2/UrQJ+I8Ob5ahypO4ONieIMMxvBRVeIJrkRP\nn10KYKEJQAAlD+4qJ8nC3rKctma4P4DKf2ROl5IsAv3VFToFMiLcL/LYUdITa4As\nM61oVQ5lpe1kYB8Skba1aREcrr12iFuk9Xq6OVt7qpCjeaYgVpnRs/gjHbXx2IFV\nWUjykiQniOXNQ/oAfhIedm95lFEr2MMrVE0aaWUo5ZZhF9p4Kz7GFYsoJoTH1eSK\n0nTW6ZTG6yfB80WJ4DTC9i2hcCmUQF+duW/3sOaM4mXsBencICQD/XD0uMRoKJDr\nYlZlIrXEGa4Q1qmR/PAv4Q8KISGSxPqm7wgYqoNzVjaYP+dZyf5AJTjT+t2E4GeJ\nyu8WXYZ2urrOZOxkeOq62WlAhqmJYDb1YfQtAFcCkXHntIZVtqwSTdYxrxxbOIDc\nQ9QpvS2gRiidyG1N3HRZ\n=/BeQ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T16:52:48Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commit/612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4","node_id":"C_kwDOT_fY69oAKDYxMmM3NTBkYTZmMjYzNDRmYWU0N2Y3NWM1MWQxYjkxZDQ3Zjk3YzQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commit/f56c7dfe7e3a31551393e64e99138f2929eb0727","sha":"f56c7dfe7e3a31551393e64e99138f2929eb0727","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/f56c7dfe7e3a31551393e64e99138f2929eb0727"}],"sha":"612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/612c750da6f26344fae47f75c51d1b91d47f97c4"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11871778?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/ValeriaMaltseva","id":11871778,"login":"ValeriaMaltseva","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExODcxNzc4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ValeriaMaltseva","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/commits/f56c7dfe7e3a31551393e64e99138f2929eb0727/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T11:31:10Z","email":"valeriia.maltseva@pimcore.com","name":"Valeriia Maltseva"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T11:31:10Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"[Studio] Ship frontend build as a committed archive (extracted at cache warmup) (#28)\n\n* [Studio] Ship frontend build as a committed archive (extracted at cache warmup)\n\nReplaces the committed, expanded public/studio/build/ tree with a single\ncontent-addressed build-dist/build-<id>.zip that studio-ui-bundle''s\nBuildArchiveExtractionTrait reconstructs at cache warmup. 23 committed build\nfiles become one 668 KB archive, removing the per-build file churn and merge\nconflicts the committed build used to cause.\n\n- WebpackEntryPointProvider implements BuildArchiveProviderInterface via the\n  shared trait; the interface_exists() guard is dropped since studio-ui-bundle\n  is a hard composer requirement. Both entry point accessors gain @return\n  string[], since dropping the guard makes the class visible to PHPStan.\n- rsbuild.config.ts derives the build id from source content (getBuildGroupId +\n  pluginWriteBuildId) instead of a random uuid, and the clean-up step keeps the\n  current build dir because studio-package-build picks the lexicographically\n  greatest .build-id on disk. uuid stays in package.json: it is a runtime\n  dependency and part of the Module Federation shared map.\n- Adds a Studio Frontend Build workflow. This repo had none, so the archive\n  would otherwise never be regenerated. It uses the packaged reusable workflow\n  with lint disabled (no lint script here) and tests enabled.\n- No npm change: @pimcore/studio-ui-bundle is a 2026.3 canary, well past the\n  ^2026.2.1 that first shipped the Build/* classes, ./bundler/build-id,\n  pluginWriteBuildId and the studio-package-build CLI — all verified present in\n  the pinned canary. package-lock.json is untouched.\n- composer tightens pimcore/studio-ui-bundle from ^2026.2 to ^2026.2.1, since\n  ^2026.2 still admitted 2026.2.0, which predates the Build/* classes.\n\nSee https://github.com/pimcore/platform-version/issues/247\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Update studio-frontend-build workflow parameters\n\nRemoved lint and test parameters from studio-frontend-build job.\n\n* Enable testing and disable linting in build workflow\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"fb06ac3b98f16a24bd08b1cf8131b32e1e879931","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/git/trees/fb06ac3b98f16a24bd08b1cf8131b32e1e879931"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pimcore/pimcore-wysiwyg-editor/git/commits/f56c7dfe7e3a31551393e64e99138f2929eb0727","verification":{"payload":"tree fb06ac3b98f16a24bd08b1cf8131b32e1e879931\nparent 6b05bd0d881293d3d0480d60bea2739593399847\nauthor Valeriia Maltseva <valeriia.maltseva@pimcore.com> 1789990270 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789990270 +0200\n\n[Studio] Ship frontend build as a committed archive (extracted at cache warmup) (#28)\n\n* [Studio] Ship frontend build as a committed archive (extracted at cache warmup)\n\nReplaces the committed, expanded public/studio/build/ tree with a single\ncontent-addressed build-dist/build-<id>.zip that studio-ui-bundle''s\nBuildArchiveExtractionTrait reconstructs at cache warmup. 23 committed build\nfiles become one 668 KB archive, removing the per-build file churn and merge\nconflicts the committed build used to cause.\n\n- WebpackEntryPointProvider implements BuildArchiveProviderInterface via the\n  shared trait; the interface_exists() guard is dropped since studio-ui-bundle\n  is a hard composer requirement. Both entry point accessors gain @return\n  string[], since dropping the guard makes the class visible to PHPStan.\n- rsbuild.config.ts derives the build id from source content (getBuildGroupId +\n  pluginWriteBuildId) instead of a random uuid, and the clean-up step keeps the\n  current build dir because studio-package-build picks the lexicographically\n  greatest .build-id on disk. uuid stays in package.json: it is a runtime\n  dependency and part of the Module Federation shared map.\n- Adds a Studio Frontend Build workflow. This repo had none, so the archive\n  would otherwise never be regenerated. It uses the packaged reusable workflow\n  with lint disabled (no lint script here) and tests enabled.\n- No npm change: @pimcore/studio-ui-bundle is a 2026.3 canary, well past the\n  ^2026.2.1 that first shipped the Build/* classes, ./bundler/build-id,\n  pluginWriteBuildId and the studio-package-build CLI — all verified present in\n  the pinned canary. package-lock.json is untouched.\n- composer tightens pimcore/studio-ui-bundle from ^2026.2 to ^2026.2.1, since\n  ^2026.2 still admitted 2026.2.0, which predates the Build/* classes.\n\nSee https://github.com/pimcore/platform-version/issues/247\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Update studio-frontend-build workflow parameters\n\nRemoved lint and test parameters from studio-frontend-build job.\n\n* Enable testing and disable linting in build workflow\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsRV+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmYEQAHUi5eOHfSz7l2i2MUL9cwrk\ni/6Q6FpkyasItrWgkDT0nnuheUMOv3NDJpKiS7REDVtiSdgUD0pzp36DXR0D6zF/\n9KJVB1ZK9MtLDIEI0RwG/Z6SqIfY46QGkUoLkPBPwBMUKZwnAo691C0Pcjbw9fg7\nFuiBTfcVoBzPElWRlNAey8XboAE1CTIUDTgGczBwR1F/pSqkYQFDC1VkHXMaEgtC\nuYx6XEeLtZAZGnwiayoVSCDHdI4Sn5Nsy+K4B+XwkcgmLUtv9Zlea8tSj+xI1nz3\nzvgoqMhIMkLcnaZ7WRNLr2PH+jEXYnulhliCLaq77zyEvQu+GQDLiuFS/T3fkz5V\nmjoQNnKneWKMPaHK8mT1YGIH73A4A6zTEH0DUzKb/nFLL8sCDO+QO/bMgiDJnyeM\nZ5zg/4+uPGmDDi+lghD9/nnb9Ud7z+3F6UHbYthGyFhs7smWhSliJPDdpcrhbxQp\n2q/xOVC1C9L0F71+fabQGA0OmD